::GIMP Text Layer management::
In GIMP, how does a text layer differ from standard image layers during the design process? 
[difficulty:medium]
[chapter:Graphics in ICT]
[tags:image_editing,gimp,text]
{
    =It allows the designer to insert and edit text characters dynamically without affecting other parts of the artwork or background layer
    ~It cannot be moved, resized, or rotated once it is created on the canvas
    ~It automatically merges with the background layer, making it impossible to edit later
    ~It is a non-transparent solid color sheet that covers the entire image size
}

::Layer Group Purpose::
Why are Layer Groups used in complex 2D image design projects that contain a large number of separate layers? 
[difficulty:medium]
[chapter:Graphics in ICT]
[tags:image_editing,gimp,layers]
{
    =To keep the workspace neat and organized by grouping layers into a hierarchical structure, allowing multiple layers to be moved, hidden, or modified at once
    ~To permanently lock all image layers so that no further edits can be made by the user
    ~To reduce the file size of the project by merging all image layers into a single pixel layer
    ~To automatically convert 2D raster layers into 3D vector graphics
}

::GIMP 3D Text Shadow Effect::
In GIMP, how is a simple 3D drop-shadow text effect created using filters? 
[difficulty:medium]
[chapter:Graphics in ICT]
[tags:image_editing,gimp,3d_text]
{
    =By adding text, navigating to Filters \(\rightarrow\) Light and Shadow \(\rightarrow\) Drop Shadow, and adjusting the offset and blur radius
    ~By drawing a square on the workplane and dropping a cylinder inside it
    ~By using the Eraser tool to carve a shadow outline on the text layer by hand
    ~By adding a Layer Mask and painting it entirely with black brush strokes
}

::GIMP 3D Shape Bevel Effect::
To make a flat 2D rectangle selection filled with color look like a raised 3D button, what GIMP filter sequence and adjustment should be applied? 
[difficulty:medium]
[chapter:Graphics in ICT]
[tags:image_editing,gimp,3d_shapes]
{
    =Click Filters \(\rightarrow\) Decor \(\rightarrow\) Add Bevel and adjust the thickness parameter as needed
    ~Click Colors \(\rightarrow\) Brightness-Contrast and maximize the brightness of the rectangle
    ~Click Layer \(\rightarrow\) New Layer Group and rename the group to \"Button\"
    ~Click Edit \(\rightarrow\) Cut and then paste the selection as a new text layer
}

::Historical Animation Frame Volume::
Historically, why was hand-drawn traditional animation considered an incredibly big job? Depending on quality, how many frames are typically required for one minute of animation? 
[difficulty:medium]
[chapter:Graphics in ICT]
[tags:animation,history]
{
    =Between \(720\) and \(1800\) separate still images
    ~Between \(10\) and \(50\) separate still images
    ~Exactly \(100\) still images
    ~Over \(10000\) still images per second
}

::Animation Principle Squash and Stretch::
When a bouncing rubber ball hits the ground, it squashes flat, and then stretches vertically as it bounces back up. Which fundamental principle of animation does this exemplify? 
[difficulty:medium]
[chapter:Graphics in ICT]
[tags:animation,principles]
{
    =Squash and Stretch
    ~Anticipation
    ~Timing
    ~Follow Through
}

::Animation Principle Anticipation::
Before a person jumps, they bend their knees. Before an athlete throws a ball, they pull their arm back. Which animation principle uses a small action before a big action so the audience knows what is coming? 
[difficulty:medium]
[chapter:Graphics in ICT]
[tags:animation,principles]
{
    =Anticipation
    ~Ease In and Ease Out
    ~Timing
    ~Follow Through
}

::Animation Principle Timing::
According to the animation principle of Timing, how does the number of drawing frames change depending on the speed of an action (e.g., a slow walk vs. a fast run)? 
[difficulty:medium]
[chapter:Graphics in ICT]
[tags:animation,principles]
{
    =Moving fast requires fewer drawing frames, while moving slowly requires more drawing frames
    ~Moving fast requires more drawing frames, while moving slowly requires fewer drawing frames
    ~Both fast and slow movements require the exact same number of drawing frames
    ~Slow movements do not require any drawing frames; they are done automatically by the software
}

::Animation Software Options::
Match the animation software with its description: Which free and open-source software is widely used professionally for 3D modeling, rigging, rendering, and complex 3D animations? 
[difficulty:medium]
[chapter:Graphics in ICT]
[tags:animation,software]
{
    =Blender
    ~Adobe Animate
    ~Pencil2D
    ~Microsoft PowerPoint
}

::Relative Cell Referencing::
What is the default type of cell referencing in spreadsheets, and how does it behave when a formula is copied to another cell? 
[difficulty:medium]
[chapter:Electronic Spreadsheet (Extended)]
[tags:spreadsheet,referencing]
{
    =Relative Referencing; the cell addresses in the formula change automatically relative to where the formula is copied
    ~Absolute Referencing; the cell addresses remain completely fixed and do not change
    ~Mixed Referencing; only the column changes while the row remains locked
    ~Fixed Referencing; the formula is cleared and must be typed again manually
}

::Absolute Cell Referencing::
A student wants to multiply item prices by a fixed tax rate located in cell B1. They want to copy this formula down the column, ensuring the tax rate cell reference does not change. Which character must they use, and what is the correct absolute reference representation? 
[difficulty:medium]
[chapter:Electronic Spreadsheet (Extended)]
[tags:spreadsheet,referencing]
{
    =The \$ symbol; represented as \$B\$1
    ~The \# symbol; represented as \#B\#1
    ~The \& symbol; represented as \&B\&1
    ~The \@ symbol; represented as \@B\@1
}

::Linking Cells between Different Sheets::
A cricket coach records match runs in two different worksheets named Match1 and Match2. To calculate total runs in Sheet3, they want to link B2 from Match1 and B2 from Match2. What is the correct syntax of the linking formula? 
[difficulty:medium]
[chapter:Electronic Spreadsheet (Extended)]
[tags:spreadsheet,linking]
{
    =\=Match1!B2\+Match2!B2
    =\=Match1:B2\+Match2:B2
    =\=Match1\#B2\+Match2\#B2
    =\=Match1.B2\+Match2.B2
}

::Chart Type Selection continuous trends::
A student records temperature readings hourly throughout the day and wants to visualize how the temperature rises and falls. Which chart type is best suited to show continuous changes or trends in data over a period of time? 
[difficulty:medium]
[chapter:Electronic Spreadsheet (Extended)]
[tags:spreadsheet,charts]
{
    =Line Chart
    ~Pie Chart
    ~Bar Chart
    ~Histogram
}

::Chart Type Selection circular parts of whole::
A company wants to display how its monthly budget is distributed among rent, salaries, utilities, and marketing as percentages of a whole. Which chart type is circular, divided into slices, and has a total slice sum of 100%? 
[difficulty:medium]
[chapter:Electronic Spreadsheet (Extended)]
[tags:spreadsheet,charts]
{
    =Pie Chart
    ~Line Chart
    ~Scatter Chart
    ~Histogram
}

::Supervised vs Unsupervised Learning::
What is the core difference between Supervised Learning and Unsupervised Learning in machine learning models? 
[difficulty:medium]
[chapter:Introduction to AI]
[tags:machine_learning,concepts]
{
    =Supervised learning uses labeled training data where a teacher gives the correct answers, while unsupervised learning groups unlabeled data on its own by finding patterns
    ~Supervised learning is only used for text, while unsupervised learning is only used for images
    ~Supervised learning is performed by humans on paper, while unsupervised learning runs entirely on a computer
    ~Supervised learning requires constant internet access, while unsupervised learning works completely offline
}

::AI Ethics Morals 4 Pillars::
A school uses an AI program to select students for a sports team. Because past team photos featured mostly tall 8th graders, the AI automatically rejects a fast, highly skilled 6th grader because of their height. Which pillar of AI morals has been violated? 
[difficulty:medium]
[chapter:Introduction to AI]
[tags:ai_ethics,morals]
{
    =Fairness and Bias
    ~Privacy
    ~Accountability
    ~Transparency
}

::AI Ethics Morals Privacy Pillar::
A student writes personal thoughts in a journal app. A week later, they begin seeing targeted advertisements on their phone selling products related to their entries because the app shared their private data. Which moral pillar did this app violate? 
[difficulty:medium]
[chapter:Introduction to AI]
[tags:ai_ethics,privacy]
{
    =Privacy
    ~Fairness
    ~Accountability
    ~Transparency
}

::AI Ethics Morals Accountability Pillar::
A self-driving car confuses a weird shadow and scratches a parked car. The car manufacturer, the software engineers, and the owner sitting in the driver's seat argue over who is responsible. This represents a central challenge in which ethical pillar? 
[difficulty:medium]
[chapter:Introduction to AI]
[tags:ai_ethics,accountability]
{
    =Accountability
    ~Transparency
    ~Fairness
    ~Privacy
}

::AI Ethics Morals Transparency Pillar::
An AI grading system gives a student a poor grade on a science project. When the student asks why, the teacher replies, \"I have no idea; the computer's math is a black box we cannot view.\" This failure represents a violation of which AI moral pillar? 
[difficulty:medium]
[chapter:Introduction to AI]
[tags:ai_ethics,transparency]
{
    =Transparency (Explainability)
    ~Privacy
    ~Fairness
    ~Accountability
}

::Natural Language Processing NLP::
Computers do not speak human languages directly. How does Natural Language Processing (NLP) help a school chatbot process a sentence like \"I have a terrible headache today\"? 
[difficulty:medium]
[chapter:Introduction to AI]
[tags:ai_basics,nlp]
{
    =It turns the human sentence into math, ignores common filler words, and flags the keyword \"headache\" to trigger the pre-programmed response
    ~It automatically translates the text into binary code and stores it in Google Drive
    ~It uses a spreadsheet formula to calculate how many characters are in the text
    ~It opens an online Jitsi Meet video call with a nurse immediately
}

::MIT App Inventor Visible vs Non-Visible::
In the MIT App Inventor Designer workspace, what is the primary difference between components like Buttons and Text Labels versus components like Sound players or GPS sensors? 
[difficulty:medium]
[chapter:Introduction to AI]
[tags:app_inventor,components]
{
    =Buttons and Text Labels are Visible components that the user can see and touch on the screen, while Sound players and GPS are Non-Visible components that run in the background
    ~Buttons and Text Labels cannot be programmed in the Blocks Editor, while Sound players and GPS can
    ~Non-Visible components appear as colorful visual puzzle pieces on the phone mock-up, while Visible components do not
    ~Visible components are only used for games, while Non-Visible components are only used for math formulas
}

::MIT App Inventor Meow App Logic::
To program a simple app where a picture of a cat plays a \"meow\" sound when tapped, which connected block combination must be built in the Blocks Editor? 
[difficulty:medium]
[chapter:Introduction to AI]
[tags:app_inventor,programming_logic]
{
    =An Event block \`When Image1.Touched do...\` containing an Action block \`Call Sound1.Play\` inside
    ~A text block \`print(\"meow\")\` inside an input function box
    ~A mixed referencing formula block \`=Image1*Sound1\` from a spreadsheet
    ~An absolute referencing block \`\$Image\$1\` and an \`unhide\` block
}

::Prompt Engineer Career::
A Prompt Engineer is an emerging career of the AI era. What does a Prompt Engineer do? 
[difficulty:medium]
[chapter:Introduction to AI]
[tags:ai_careers,prompt_engineering]
{
    =Crafts highly specific, structured text instructions and phrasing to talk to AI models to generate the highest quality text, code, or image outputs
    ~Designs physical mechanical gear components and structural 3D prints in Tinkercad
    ~Maintains and repairs high-voltage physical hardware lines and circuits in smart factories
    ~Monitors bank accounts to ensure all automated online loan transactions are completed successfully
}

