::Layer Mask Definition::
What is a Layer Mask in image editing software like GIMP? 
[difficulty:hard]
[chapter:Graphics in ICT]
[tags:image_editing,gimp,layer_mask]
{
    =A transparency mask attached to a layer that defines what parts of that layer are transparent or opaque, allowing non-destructive editing
    ~A protective tool that prevents any unauthorized users from editing or viewing specific layer details
    ~A solid-color background layer that is automatically added when a new file is opened
    ~A tool used to draw perfect geometric shapes like circles and rectangles on a text layer
}

::Layer Mask Simple Rule::
According to the simple rules of Layer Masks, how do the colors black, white, and gray affect layer visibility? 
[difficulty:hard]
[chapter:Graphics in ICT]
[tags:image_editing,gimp,layer_mask]
{
    =White shows the layer (full opacity), Black hides the layer (full transparency), and Gray partially hides or blends the layer
    ~Black shows the layer (full opacity), White hides the layer (full transparency), and Gray has no effect
    ~White shows the layer, Gray hides the layer, and Black partially blends the layer
    ~Gray shows the layer, Black hides the layer, and White makes it fully transparent
}

::Eraser vs Layer Mask::
What is the key advantage of using a Layer Mask over the Eraser tool in digital art and image blending? 
[difficulty:hard]
[chapter:Graphics in ICT]
[tags:image_editing,gimp,layer_mask]
{
    =The Eraser tool is destructive because it permanently deletes pixels, while a Layer Mask is non-destructive because pixels are only hidden and can be restored anytime
    ~The Eraser tool is non-destructive, while a Layer Mask permanently changes the image pixels
    ~A Layer Mask is easier for quick permanent cleanup, while the Eraser tool allows for smooth blending
    ~There is no functional difference; both permanently delete the original pixels of the image
}

::GIMP 3D Text Duplicate Layer Effect::
To create a realistic thick 3D text effect using duplicate layers in GIMP, what steps should a student follow after typing their text? 
[difficulty:hard]
[chapter:Graphics in ICT]
[tags:image_editing,gimp,3d_text]
{
    =Duplicate the text layer, change the bottom layer's color to a darker shade using Brightness-Contrast, and use the Move tool to slightly shift the bottom layer down and right
    ~Duplicate the text layer, merge it with the background layer, and delete the original text layer
    ~Duplicate the text layer and apply a black layer mask to the top text layer
    ~Duplicate the text layer and use the Eraser tool to remove alternate letters from the bottom layer
}

::Mixed Referencing Formula::
In a mixed cell reference such as C\$5 or \$C5, what is the functional effect of the \$ symbol when the formula is copied to other rows or columns? 
[difficulty:hard]
[chapter:Electronic Spreadsheet (Extended)]
[tags:spreadsheet,referencing]
{
    =It locks and fixes only one part of the cell reference (either the column or the row) while leaving the other part relative
    ~It converts the cell value into a currency format with a dollar sign
    ~It locks both the column and the row simultaneously so neither can change
    ~It indicates that the cell contains a mathematical formula instead of text
}

::Cell Referencing Identification::
Using a spreadsheet to calculate a multiplication table, a student enters the formula \=B\$1\*\$A2 in cell B2. Which type of referencing is being used for both B\$1 and \$A2? 
[difficulty:hard]
[chapter:Electronic Spreadsheet (Extended)]
[tags:spreadsheet,referencing]
{
    =Mixed Referencing
    ~Relative Referencing
    ~Absolute Referencing
    ~Circular Referencing
}

::Linking Cells between Workbooks::
What is the general formula syntax used in LibreOffice Calc to link and pull data from cell B2 in Sheet1 of an external spreadsheet file named \"Match scores.ods\"? 
[difficulty:hard]
[chapter:Electronic Spreadsheet (Extended)]
[tags:spreadsheet,linking]
{
    =\='file:///path/Match scores.ods'\#\$Sheet1.B2
    =\=[Match scores.ods]Sheet1!B2
    =\=Match scores.ods\#Sheet1:B2
    =\=Link('Match scores.ods', Sheet1, B2)
}

::Scatter XY Chart Purpose::
In a science project, a student wants to plot study hours on the X-axis against student test scores on the Y-axis to see if there is a relationship or correlation between the two. Which chart type plots data points as individual dots using two numerical values? 
[difficulty:hard]
[chapter:Electronic Spreadsheet (Extended)]
[tags:spreadsheet,charts]
{
    =Scatter (XY) Chart
    ~Column Chart
    ~Area Chart
    ~Histogram
}

::Histogram Characteristics::
What are the unique structural features of a Histogram that distinguish it from a standard vertical Column Chart? 
[difficulty:hard]
[chapter:Electronic Spreadsheet (Extended)]
[tags:spreadsheet,charts]
{
    =It is used to show the frequency distribution of continuous numerical data grouped into class intervals (ranges) along the X-axis, and the vertical bars touch with no gaps
    ~It uses horizontal side-to-side rectangular bars where the length of each bar represents a category ranking
    ~It represents values as slices of a circle that must always add up to exactly 100%
    ~It connects individual data points with straight lines to show changes over time
}

::Machine Mistakes Context Blindness::
Why might a highly advanced AI system in a self-driving car stop suddenly when encountering a pedestrian's reflection in a store window, whereas a human driver would easily continue? 
[difficulty:hard]
[chapter:Introduction to AI]
[tags:machine_mistakes,context_blindness]
{
    =The AI lacks common sense and real-world context (context blindness) and relies strictly on mathematical image patterns without understanding reality
    ~The AI was trying to please the passenger by showing off its braking speed
    ~The store window reflection was too hot for the car's temperature sensors
    ~The AI model suffered a chatbot hallucination and made up a fictional pedestrian
}

::Data Cleaning temperature list::
A student collects school temperature readings: Monday: 32°C, Tuesday: 33°C, Wednesday: -400°C, Thursday: \"Hot\", Friday: 31°C. To clean this dataset before using it to train an AI model, what actions should they take? 
[difficulty:hard]
[chapter:Introduction to AI]
[tags:data_science,data_cleaning]
{
    =Delete the Wednesday entry (-400°C is physically impossible) and the Thursday entry (\"Hot\" is a text formatting error) to preserve only valid numeric data
    ~Average all five values, including Wednesday and Thursday, to find the median temperature
    ~Convert the entire dataset into a Pie chart to automatically hide impossible numbers
    ~Convert the text \"Hot\" into a number 1000 and multiply it by Wednesday's temperature
}

::L3 Multi-Sheet Formula Linking 1::
If you are linking sheets in LibreOffice Calc, what does the external reference formula \(=Sheet1!B4 + Sheet2!B4\) compute?
[difficulty:hard]
[chapter:Electronic Spreadsheet (Extended)]
[tags:spreadsheet,linking,math]
{
    =The sum of cell B4 in Sheet1 and Sheet2
    ~The quotient of cell B4 divided by Sheet1
    ~The product of cell B4 across all active workbooks
    ~The name of the active worksheet
}

